lane: feed-staged-loop
why: after the linear view, the feed still builds TWO Free nodes per
  element -- c.send(a) and the flatMap around it -- and both are
  interpreted. The shape is fully known at the point of writing: "while
  there are elements, send". That is exactly the condition under which
  this library already stages: inline combinators fold a known
  pipeline into one while-loop, measured at 1.6us against 16.9 for the
  chunked interpreter on the same work.
how: the feed runs on a forked fiber, on a virtual thread, where
  CanBlock is granted -- so it can be ONE Async.Run holding a plain
  loop over sendBlocking, instead of a program of 2N nodes. One Free
  node for the whole feed.
the cost, named up front: CANCELLATION GRANULARITY. Today a fiber can
  be cancelled between elements because the interpreter runs between
  them; inside a loop, cancellation is noticed only at the blocking
  send, through interruption. That is a semantic change, so it needs
  saying in the docs and a test that a cancelled feed actually stops.
land only if measured faster than 19.7 on the chunk-native lane, and
  only with the cancellation behaviour tested rather than assumed.
